---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Principles | Amitoj Singh" description="Four working distinctions about values, evidence, capability, rules, and measurement, followed by a personal Sikh mapping for knowledge creation.">
  <!-- Hero Photo -->
  <section class="container-zen pt-12 pb-8">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <img
        src="/images/photography-optimized/DSCF0807.webp"
        alt="Tennis court in autumn"
        class="w-full h-72 object-cover"
        style="object-position: center center;"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen pt-8">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h1 class="mb-8 text-balance">Principles</h1>

      <div class="accent-line mb-8"></div>

      <p class="text-lg text-ink-light mb-6">
        “Principles” makes these sound more settled than they are. I return to them when a decision feels confused and I need to locate the source of the confusion.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-light">
        The same decision can fail in different places: in the value it serves, in the method used to pursue it, or in a rule that has outlived its reason. I use the distinctions below to keep those failures from borrowing one another’s authority.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <div class="space-y-12">

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">What matters comes before what can be measured</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            A measure needs an object. Before data can tell me whether something improved, someone has already chosen what counts as improvement. That choice may be revised, but it cannot be supplied by the measurement created to serve it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Evidence can test whether the method served that choice. It cannot make the choice disappear. When the value itself is contested, the disagreement still belongs to the people and interests at stake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">A capability is a claim under conditions</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            Saying “I can do this” is not an identity. It is a claim about what I can repeat under named conditions, including the conditions under which I fail.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            I treat autonomy the same way. More freedom should follow demonstrated judgment and faster feedback, not confidence stated in advance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">A rule should preserve a judgment, not bury it</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            When a decision recurs, codifying it saves attention. The gain becomes a risk when the instruction survives after the reason for it has changed.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            I use a rule where the same condition should produce the same action. I keep a principle where the condition must still be interpreted.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            The edge case is the test. If following the instruction correctly produces the result it was meant to prevent, the premise needs to be reopened.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">A metric changes what becomes visible</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-light mb-4">
            A metric begins as a report about attention. Once it enters a review or target, it also redirects attention. What it captures becomes easier to defend than what it omits.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            The trouble begins when the proxy inherits the authority of the value it was built to serve.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-8 section-header">A personal Sikh mapping</h2>

      <p class="text-ink-light mb-8">
        I use three Sikh practices as a personal sequence for knowledge creation: Naam Japna as epistemic humility, Kirat Karna as the work of testing, and Vandh Chakna as sharing what others can test and extend. This is my mapping, not a definition of the tradition.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Naam Japna &mdash; epistemic humility</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Naam Japna reminds me that reality is not obliged to fit my model. I begin by leaving room for correction.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Kirat Karna &mdash; the work of testing</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Kirat Karna moves the claim into work. Choose a method, state the conditions, run the trial, and account for the result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl mb-4 text-sage-700">Vandh Chakna &mdash; sharing what others can test and extend</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-light">
            Vandh Chakna moves the result beyond me. Share the artifact, the method, and the credit so another person can test or extend the claim.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-zen section-zen border-t border-oat-300 bg-oat-100">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl mb-6">Continue</h2>

      <p class="text-ink-light mb-8">
        Research and writing show where these distinctions enter the rest of the work.
      </p>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
        <a href="/research" class="arrow-link">Research</a>
        <a href="/writing" class="arrow-link">Writing</a>
        <a href="/about" class="arrow-link">About</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
